# the argument of aristotle-chief-good.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# "it is thought" reports common opinion with no named author, and the text argues from it, so it is a plain premise.
# The conclusion says calling the Chief Good "that which all things aim at" is a fair description.
# I read that as: there is some good at which all these pursuits aim.
# The step moves from "each aims at some good" to "some good is aimed at by all" (order of all and some).
atom	art(x)	x is an art
atom	science(x)	x is a science reduced to a teachable form
atom	action(x)	x is an action
atom	choice(x)	x is a moral choice
atom	good(y)	y is a good
atom	aims(x, y)	x aims at y
P1	S1	∀x (art(x) ∨ science(x) ∨ action(x) ∨ choice(x) → ∃y (good(y) ∧ aims(x, y)))
C	S1	∃y (good(y) ∧ ∀x (art(x) ∨ science(x) ∨ action(x) ∨ choice(x) → aims(x, y)))	from P1
